# Model card — MiniMol/AmpC encoder

Built {built_date} from `{git_sha}`, checkpoint sha256 `{ckpt_sha}`.

## What it is

MiniMol v1's full trunk — 16 message-passing GNN layers over the molecular graph, a virtual
node updated 15 times, two positional encoders, **{trunk_params:,} parameters** — fine-tuned
end to end on AmpC docking data, with a {head_params:,}-parameter joint classification +
regression head on top.

The **deliverable is the encoder**, not the predictions. The predictions are the training
signal that shaped the representation.

| | |
|---|---|
| export | `pooled512` — `global_max_pool` over the final GNN layer, 512-d |
| also available | `z` — the head's shared layer, {embed_dim}-d, **not** recommended (README, "Which vector to use") |
| predictions | pProp (regression) and a logit for pProp ≥ {pprop_edge} |

## The target

`pprop = -log10(rank_max / 1e7)`, a quantile of the AmpC docking score over a 10M-molecule
library. **Higher = more potent.** It caps at **7.0** (rank 1 of 10M).

Note the sign convention flips relative to the raw docking score, which is in kcal/mol where
*more negative* is better. pProp is monotone in it.

The binary threshold is **pProp ≥ {pprop_edge}** — the point at which binders become possible,
and the finest split leaving a learnable positive class: 3,153 molecules of 331,480 (~0.95%).
At pProp ≥ 5.0 there are only 100 in the whole dataset.

## Training data

{n_train:,} molecules from `ampc_subset_331k.csv` — a subsample of a 10M AmpC docking library
that keeps the **entire potent tail** and subsamples the bulk. Every molecule in the library
with pProp ≥ 2.5 is present, so the positive class is a census rather than a sample. SMILES are
RDKit-canonical (isomeric) and deduplicated.

The subset is therefore **~30× tail-enriched by construction** relative to the library.

## How it was trained

Hyperparameters were selected by a 131-configuration Bayesian sweep (`lyc0lh2d`), each
configuration scored as the mean over a full 5-fold × 2-seed grid on cluster-CV splits. The
top three were a statistical tie (0.09 σ apart); this checkpoint uses the second, refit.

Two phases with independent lengths and one cosine anneal each: the head trains alone on the
frozen trunk, then the trunk unfreezes and both train together.

| | |
|---|---|
| freeze / unfrozen epochs | {freeze_epochs} / {unfrozen_epochs} |
| head LR (phase 1 / phase 2) | {head_lr:.3e} / {head_lr_unfrozen:.3e} |
| trunk LR (phase 2) | {trunk_lr:.3e} |
| batch size | {batch_size} |
| loss weighting | `balanced` — two-group inverse frequency at pProp {pprop_edge} (104× ratio) |
| target normalization | `{pprop_norm}`, statistics from the training set; predictions are denormalized back to raw pProp |
| head | `512 → {embed_dim}` (Linear + LayerNorm + GELU), then two bare `Linear({embed_dim} → 1)` |

The freeze was asserted, not assumed: across the frozen phase the trunk was bit-for-bit
unchanged (Δ = {frozen_trunk_delta:.3e}) while the head provably moved (Δ =
{frozen_head_delta:.3e}), and the trunk moved once unfrozen (Δ = {unfrozen_trunk_delta:.3e}).

## ⚠ These weights were refit on ALL the data

**This checkpoint was trained on all {n_train:,} molecules, with nothing held out.** That is a
deliberate refit-after-selection: the hyperparameters were chosen with cross-validation, and
the shipped model then uses every molecule available.

Two consequences you have to design around:

1. **No held-out metric exists for these exact weights.** The numbers below come from ten
   *identically configured* models that did hold a fold out. The run's own `val/*` metrics are
   in-sample and are stamped `"val_is_in_sample": true` in `model/meta.json`; do not quote them.

2. **Every molecule in `ampc_subset_331k.csv` is in-sample for this model.** An
   active-learning benchmark scored on molecules drawn from that subset will read
   optimistically, and the size of the optimism is not measured. Score on library molecules
   **outside** the 331k subset — the other ~9.67M — or accept and state the bias.

If you need a model with an honest held-out set, the same configuration trained with fold 0
held out is a ~10-minute run in the source repository.

## Performance

{n_siblings} models at this configuration, each trained on a bootstrap resample of fold 0's
training side, scored on fold 0's **held-out** {sib_n_val:,} molecules ({sib_n_pos:,} positive
at pProp >= {pprop_edge}). Mean +/- sd over the {n_siblings}:

| metric | value |
|---|---|
| Pearson r (pProp) | **{m_pearson}** |
| Spearman rho | {m_spearman} |
| R^2 | {m_r2} |
| MAE (raw pProp units) | {m_mae} |
| Average precision, pProp >= {pprop_edge} | **{m_ap}** (base rate {sib_base_rate:.4f}) |
| Enrichment factor @ top 1% | {m_ef1} |
| Enrichment factor @ top 0.1% | {m_ef01} |
| composite `goal_metric` | {m_goal} |

Those {n_siblings} each saw ~167k unique of 265,184 training rows (a bootstrap keeps ~63.2%
distinct), against this checkpoint's {n_train:,}. So they are a **conservative** read on it —
but a read on a *sibling*, not a measurement of these weights.

Regression quality is very uneven across the range, and this matters for acquisition: within
the bulk (pProp < {pprop_edge}) Pearson is {m_pearson_lt}, but **within the potent group
(pProp >= {pprop_edge}) it is {m_pearson_ge}** and Spearman {m_spearman_ge}. The model ranks
potent-vs-bulk well and ranks *within* the potent tail poorly.

## Scope — what this model can and cannot claim

- **Generalisation to new clusters within this library, not to new chemistry.** The CV split
  is ECFP4/2048 sphere-exclusion clustering at Tanimoto distance 0.65, with whole clusters
  dealt into folds. That removes analog leakage (molecules with a training neighbour ≥ 0.7
  similarity drop 5.15% → 2.11%) but barely moves the median nearest-neighbour similarity. It
  is a harder split than random, and it is not a test of novel chemistry.
- **One target, one protein.** AmpC β-lactamase docking scores. Nothing here transfers to
  another target without measurement.
- **Docking scores, not activity.** The label is a docking program's output, with all of that
  method's biases; it is not experimental binding.
- **The metrics above describe this ~30× tail-enriched subset**, not the 10M library. There is
  no reweighting in this package that corrects back to library base rates, so read the
  enrichment factors as subset numbers.
- **`pooled512` is not a MiniMol embedding.** Fine-tuning cost it 54% of its structural
  correlation with ECFP4 Tanimoto similarity and 28% of its effective rank. It is a
  pProp-shaped space now.

